---
import "@void-drift/core/styles/buttons.css";
import "@void-drift/core/styles/surfaces.css";
import ControllerGeometryBench from "../../../components/studies/in-game-controllers/ControllerGeometryBench.svelte";
import StageMarginBench from "../../../components/studies/in-game-controllers/StageMarginBench.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((item) => item.slug === "in-game-controllers");
if (!study) throw new Error("Missing study metadata for in-game-controllers");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="part-01">
		<h2 id="part-01">
			01 / Side-margin 60° thrust flaps across thumbs and keyboards
		</h2>
		<p>
			On a keyboard, a pilot flies with three direct commands: left engine (<code
				>A</code
			>
			/ <code>←</code>), right engine (<code>D</code>
			/ <code>→</code>), and a single-key forward burn (<code>W</code>
			/ <code>↑</code>) that fires both thrusters together. To give touch
			players the same single-thumb access to both turning and full forward
			thrust, the controller layout establishes four fixed givens from the
			concept draft:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Ship-free side margins:</strong>
				Both control pairs sit flush against the left and right screen edges,
				inside the outer horizontal margin where camera framing, padding, and
				the circular arena keep the ship from traveling.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Lower-corner full thrust, upper-margin turn:</strong>
				Full forward thrust (<code>F</code>, firing both engines) anchors both
				lower corners; single-engine turn (<code>L</code>
				on the left,
				<code>R</code>
				on the right) sits directly above <code>F</code> along the lower-middle
				side edge.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Stacked 60° angled flaps:</strong>
				Each side uses two overlapping 60°-angled flaps (sloping 30° downward
				from horizontal inward from the screen bezel), with the lower
				<code>F</code>
				flap tucked over the base of the upper <code>L</code> /
				<code>R</code>
				flap.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			With the locations and 60°-angled flap silhouette established, this study
			investigates how the stage envelope and side instrumentation margins are
			defined across screens, how large the touch zones should be inside those
			margins, and how the stacked flaps layer and style their states.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="part-02">
		<h2 id="part-02">02 / Exploration</h2>

		<details class="prior-iteration">
			<summary>
				02.1 / Initial raw viewport sizing (superseded by 02.2 stage envelope)
			</summary>
			<div class="details-body">
				<p>
					<strong>Observation from 02.1:</strong>
					Plotting the 60° flaps across unconstrained device viewports (<code
						>19.5:9</code
					>
					phone through
					<code>4:3</code>
					tablet and desktop) revealed that the software has not yet defined the
					allowable stage aspect-ratio bounds or separated the
					<em>rendered space area</em>
					from the
					<em>active camera framing box</em>
					and the
					<em>side instrumentation margins</em>.
				</p>
				<ControllerGeometryBench client:load />
			</div>
		</details>

		<h3 id="part-02-2">
			02.2 / Stage envelope (16:9 to 4:3), active camera box, and shared
			instrument margins
		</h3>
		<p>
			Instead of letting the stage stretch to arbitrary screen ratios, we
			constrain the playable screen envelope between <strong>16:9</strong>
			(widest allowed, <code>1920 × 1080</code> logical) and
			<strong>4:3</strong>
			(narrowest/tallest allowed,
			<code>1440 × 1080</code>
			logical), clamping wider or narrower viewports to the nearest bound.
			Across <code>16:9</code>, <code>4:3</code>, and every ratio in between,
			the stage divides into three concentric responsibilities with
			<strong>identical margins on all screen sizes</strong>:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Rendered space area (100% of stage):</strong>
				The void, starfield, and orbit rings render edge-to-edge across the
				entire <code>16:9..4:3</code> frame—including behind all margins.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Top &amp; bottom visual margins (<code>M_y</code>):</strong>
				Equal vertical padding at the top and bottom of the frame. Graphics
				render through them, keeping the active camera framing inset from the
				horizontal screen edges.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong
					>Left &amp; right instrumentation margins (<code>M_x</code>):</strong
				>
				Equal side rails on both <code>16:9</code> and <code>4:3</code> that
				house all HUD instruments and touch controls outside the active camera
				box: <strong>Hull &amp; Power</strong> + <strong>L / F flaps</strong> on
				the left, and <strong>Survival time &amp; Score</strong> +
				<strong>R / F flaps</strong>
				on the right.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong
					>Active camera area (<code>W - 2·M_x</code>
					× <code>H - 2·M_y</code>):</strong
				>
				The central box that the camera fits the ship and star inside.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			<strong>What to notice &amp; limitations:</strong>
			Use the sliders below to adjust the shared side instrumentation margin (<code
				>M_x</code
			>), the top/bottom visual margin (<code>M_y</code>), and the flap stack
			proportions on <code>16:9</code> and <code>4:3</code> side-by-side (drawn
			at equal logical height so identical margins have identical visual
			thickness). Check how much active camera width remains on <code>4:3</code>
			as <code>M_x</code> grows, and how the top HUD instruments clear the upper
			<code>L</code>
			/ <code>R</code> flap bevel.
		</p>

		<StageMarginBench client:load />
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="part-03">
		<h2 id="part-03">03 / To be developed after 02</h2>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
ul {
	max-width: var(--measure-prose);
	margin-block: 0 var(--spacing-md);
	padding-left: var(--spacing-md);
	display: grid;
	gap: var(--spacing-xs);
}

.prior-iteration {
	margin-block: var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-lg);
	border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	background: var(--color-elev-1);
}

.prior-iteration summary {
	padding: var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md);
	font-family: var(--font-family-ui);
	font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
	color: var(--color-text-muted);
	cursor: pointer;
}

.details-body {
	padding: var(--spacing-md);
	border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
}
</style>
